  "query_preview": "..It's been an interesting day with light to moderate, \nlocalized heavy rain occurring across eastern Pinal county, central \nand eastern Pima county and Santa Cruz county. CAMs have struggled \nall day with areal coverage of storms and how long they would last, \nespecially across eastern Pima county. Argh. Today is being enhanced \nby a shortwave that extended from eastern NV south to northern Baja. \nThe current showers/embedded thunder will continue to push east \nacross Graham/Greenlee/Cochise counties into this evening. Out \nacross western half of Pima county and south of the border, isolated \nstorms will be around into this evening. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Although PWATs decrease a bit across the area \nunder southwest flow aloft, there still is enough moisture around\nfor support daily chances of afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms, especially south and east of Tucson. If I had to pick\nthe most down day, it would be Tuesday.\n\nThursday into this weekend: Lots of uncertainty during this period\nassociated with how the upper air pattern evolves. Across the PacNW\nthere will be a deepening upper level trof. To our east, upper\nlevel high pressure will be building across the central plains. \nAnd well to our south, how does the track of Invest 95E, currently\ndeveloping well off the SW Mexican coast and likely becoming a \ntropical storm named Marie in the next 18 hours, impact our area\nwith increased tropical moisture. For now the NBM is showing an\nuptick monsoon storms with increased risk in flash flooding, \nespecially Friday into the weekend. Also it could end up being \nhotter than currently forecast.\n\n8-14 day outlooks (Sep 8-14)\nTemperature: Likely above normal 50-55% \nPrecipitation: Leaning above normal 41-47%",
